		which probes are installed during one sampler invocation. The value
		must be non-zero. The default is 64 megabytes.

What:		/sys/kernel/mm/numa_balancing/local_fault_window
Date:		May 2026
Contact:	Linux memory management mailing list <linux-mm@kvack.org>
Description:
		Read-write sampling-window sequence. Reading returns the current
		sequence. Writing a non-zero integer advances the sequence. Local
		and remote KLL sketches and protection counters are reset lazily when
		their first event for the new sequence arrives. A fault contributes to
		the sketches only when its protection probe was installed in the same
		sequence. Consumers read the current window snapshot before advancing
		the sequence; completed windows are not retained.

		The probe state uses one 64-bit page extension word: bits 63:32 hold
		the window sequence, bits 31:2 hold the install time in milliseconds
		modulo 2^30, and bits 1:0 hold inactive, local, remote, or internal
		arming state. Latency arithmetic is modulo 2^30 milliseconds. Probe
		state is installed only for order-0 folios.

What:		/sys/kernel/mm/numa_balancing/remote_scan_cycles
Date:		July 2026
Contact:	Linux memory management mailing list <linux-mm@kvack.org>
Description:
		Read-only cumulative count of completed normal NUMA address-space
		scan passes that processed at least one eligible remote-tier access-time
		probe opportunity. Eligible remote large folios contribute to this signal
		even though they are excluded from latency probe accounting.
		Userspace can use changes in this counter to align a sampling window
		with remote scan progress.

What:		/sys/kernel/mm/numa_balancing/fault_latency_quantiles
Date:		July 2026
Contact:	Linux memory management mailing list <linux-mm@kvack.org>
Description:
		Read-only weighted-KLL snapshot for the current sampling window.
		All values in one read come from the same copied sketch snapshot.
		The quantile_snapshot_v5 schema contains:

		schema
		window_seq
		algorithm
		value_source
		local_total
		remote_total
		local_protected_pages
		local_cancelled_pages
		local_dropped_fault_pages
		remote_protected_pages
		remote_cancelled_pages
		remote_dropped_fault_pages
		local_q75_ns
		local_cdf_lt_local_q75_ppm
		local_cdf_le_local_q75_ppm
		remote_cdf_lt_local_q75_ppm
		remote_cdf_le_local_q75_ppm

		Latency probe accounting is limited to order-0 folios. Large folios
		do not contribute to accepted, protected, cancelled, or dropped counts.
		All counts are therefore base-page counts. local_total and remote_total
		count accepted same-window fault samples. protected_pages counts unique
		per-base-page-folio protection opportunities armed in this window. When
		a normal NUMA scan revisits an eligible base-page PTE_NUMA mapping, or
		the local physical sampler revisits one of its PTE_NUMA mappings, the
		existing mapping is rearmed and counted once for the current window.
		Repeated visits in the same window do not add another opportunity.

		The probe install time in the page extension is independent of the
		folio access-time field used by Linux memory tiering. Rearming an
		existing base-page PTE_NUMA mapping does not change that Linux access
		time. Large-folio access-time handling remains the standard Linux NUMA
		balancing behavior.
		cancelled_pages counts probes known to have been invalidated in the same
		window before producing a fault sample. dropped_fault_pages counts
		fault-accounting attempts in this window that had no matching active
		probe installed by this tier in this window. Dropped faults do not
		contribute to the totals or KLLs.

		local_q75_ns is zero when there are no local samples. The CDF fields
		are integer parts per million in [0, 1000000], evaluated at
		local_q75_ns. An _lt_ field excludes samples equal to local P75; an
		_le_ field includes them.

What:		/sys/kernel/mm/numa_balancing/migration_enabled
Date:		July 2026
Contact:	Linux memory management mailing list <linux-mm@kvack.org>
Description:
		Read-write Boolean gate for NUMA-balancing page migration. Zero
		keeps normal NUMA scanning, protection faults, and latency sampling
		active while rejecting page moves. One permits migrations selected
		by the active NUMA-balancing mode.
